# Commise Web — Next.js environment variables
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your values.
# Or run `node scripts/fetch-secrets.mjs` to pull Clerk keys from AWS.
# https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/configuring/environment-variables

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required — Clerk authentication
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_your-publishable-key
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-secret-key

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required — API backend
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Declared and validated in src/config/env.ts. There is NO in-code default: an absent or blank value is a
# BUILD FAILURE, not a fallback. That is deliberate — `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined at build time, so a
# fallback here once compiled `http://localhost:3000` into the deployed preview bundle and every visitor's
# browser called its own machine.
#
# You normally do NOT need to set these locally: the committed `.env.development` already supplies the two
# localhost origins, and Next loads it for `next dev` (and the Playwright webServer) but never for
# `next build`. Set them in .env.local only to point a local session at a deployed backend.
#
# Identity/account API base origin (src/lib/identityServiceClient.ts, which src/lib/apiClient.ts reuses).
NEXT_PUBLIC_IDENTITY_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
# Recipe management API base origin (origin ONLY — the typed clients append /api/v1) consumed by RecipeServiceClient.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RECIPE_API_URL=http://localhost:3000
#
# Both are ALSO pinned explicitly by the `e2e-web` and build jobs in .github/workflows/_ci.yml. The
# Playwright suite does not depend on either origin being reachable: it intercepts with the origin-agnostic
# glob `page.route('**/api/v1/**')`, and the deliberately-unreachable server side is what
# tests/e2e/ssrPrefetch.spec.ts exercises. Point NEXT_PUBLIC_RECIPE_API_URL at a real host only for
# live-backend work (E2E_LIVE_BACKEND=1 / recipeLive.spec.ts).

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — Webhook verification (only needed if running Clerk webhooks locally)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-webhook-secret

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — Clerk routing (defaults shown)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL=/sign-in
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL=/sign-up
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL=/profile
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL=/profile

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — Sandbox PR path routing (ADR-0001)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-PR previews are served at https://sandbox.commise.app/pr-{N}/. The basePath is baked at build:
#   - On Vercel preview builds it derives automatically from VERCEL_GIT_PULL_REQUEST_ID.
#   - Off-Vercel (Docker/ECS) builds, set PREVIEW_BASE_PATH explicitly. Leave empty for production.
# PREVIEW_BASE_PATH=/pr-123
#
# Vercel project → Settings → Environment Variables (Preview scope), NOT this file. These are REQUIRED:
# a preview build with any of them missing now fails outright rather than shipping a localhost bundle.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RECIPE_API_URL_SANDBOX_TEMPLATE   = https://recipe-pr-{pr}.commise.app
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_IDENTITY_API_URL_SANDBOX_TEMPLATE = https://identity.sandbox.commise.app
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY             = the sandbox dev instance pk_test
#
# The names carry SANDBOX because the VALUES are sandbox topology — a per-PR recipe service and the shared
# sandbox identity host. Production cannot reuse either (no PR number to substitute; the identity value would
# point production at the sandbox instance), which is why it sets the plain names instead — see the PRODUCTION
# section below.
#
# ⚠️ Set the *_SANDBOX_TEMPLATE names, and DELETE any plain `NEXT_PUBLIC_RECIPE_API_URL` from the Preview
# scope — the PREVIEW scope only, not project-wide; production needs the plain name.
# Vercel variables are scoped to an ENVIRONMENT, not to a deployment, so one project-wide value cannot mean
# "this PR's recipe service" — every PR would read the same literal. That is not hypothetical: the pr-73
# preview was correct only because the host was typed in by hand, so PR 74 would have been built against
# PR 73's recipe service (live, healthy, unrelated — no error, just the wrong data), and would have broken
# the day PR 73 closed and its stack was torn down.
#
# `{pr}` is substituted per deployment at build time by scripts/previewEndpoints.ts (run from
# scripts/buildWithEndpoints.ts, which is the package's `build` script). Recipe MUST carry `{pr}` — every PR
# deploys its own recipe service. Identity MUST NOT need it: that one is the shared, persistent sandbox
# service every preview signs in against (Environment=global, never torn down). A leftover plain variable is
# overridden, not obeyed, and the build logs a warning naming it.
#
# CI (KVS seeding) needs the project-wide VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET — a CI secret, not here.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required on Vercel — PRODUCTION scope
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel project → Settings → Environment Variables, scoped to **Production**:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_IDENTITY_API_URL      = https://identity.commise.app
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_RECIPE_API_URL        = https://recipe.commise.app
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY = the PRODUCTION instance pk_live (clerk.commise.app)
#
# The preview machinery above does NOT cover production. scripts/previewEndpoints.ts returns nothing unless
# VERCEL_ENV === 'preview', so a production build reads these plain names straight from its environment and
# has no template to fall back on.
#
# ⚠️ Scope matters. The Preview note above says to DELETE the plain `NEXT_PUBLIC_*_API_URL` names — that
# applies to the PREVIEW scope ONLY. A Vercel variable with no scope set applies to ALL environments, so
# deleting one project-wide takes production down with it.
#
# This is not hypothetical: every production Vercel build failed from 35e2953c (the feature-001 merge)
# onward, because these were never set for Production. Before that merge the code carried
# `?? 'http://localhost:3000'` fallbacks and the build passed; removing those fallbacks (deliberately — see
# .env.development) converted a silent misconfiguration into an honest build failure, and production had
# nothing configured to fail over to. CI cannot catch this class of gap: the build job in
# .github/workflows/_ci.yml pins both names to localhost, so CI stays green no matter what Vercel holds.
# The only signal is a production deployment, so check one after changing these.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — Sentry observability
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client-side DSN (exposed to the browser via the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# Source-map upload (CI / Vercel build only)
SENTRY_ORG=radicle-co
SENTRY_PROJECT=commise-web
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
